\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A quartic interaction and its retained four-point function}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{On the real realization of #{\mathbb Q^3}, use coordinates
#{x,y,z}, counting pairing #{g_0=I} and the coordinate flag retaining
#{(x,y)} and then #{x}. Fix the action #{q/2+\lambda V}, where
##{\begin{aligned}
q&=x^2+(y-x)^2+(z-y)^2,\\
V&=\frac{x^4+y^4+z^4}{4!},\\
Q&=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&1\end{pmatrix},\\
Q^{-1}&=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\1&2&2\\1&2&3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The quadratic form is positive: the map
#{(x,y,z)\mapsto(x,y-x,z-y)} is invertible. The fourth tensor of
#{V} has diagonal components one and all other components zero,
with the displayed #{1/4!} convention. Simultaneous sign reversal
supplies an action of #{C_2} preserving the pairing, action and flag.}

\p{For the [fiber interaction](fdip-0046) after eliminating #{y,z},
the retained precision is #{S=1}, the scalar is #{c_C=2\pi}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
F_\lambda(x)
={}&\lambda\left(\frac{x^4}{8}+\frac{3x^2}{4}+\frac58\right)\\
&-\lambda^2\left(\frac{5x^6}{72}+\frac{53x^4}{48}\right.\\
&\hspace{38pt}\left.+\frac{97x^2}{24}+\frac{41}{24}\right)
 +O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
In particular, a pure quartic interaction is not preserved by
elimination. Adding a quadratic term and a constant still does not
close this family: the second-order sextic coefficient is nonzero.}

\proof{
\p{Under the Gaussian law conditional on #{x}, write
#{y=x+\eta} and #{z=x+\eta+\xi}, where #{\eta,\xi} are independent
standard real Gaussians. This follows directly from
#{q=x^2+\eta^2+\xi^2} and the triangular change of variables of
determinant one. Thus
##{C^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad S=1,\qquad c_C=2\pi.}
With #{a=\mathbb E[V\mid x]} and #{b=\operatorname{Var}(V\mid x)},
use #{\mathbb E\eta^{2k}=(2k-1)!!}, with #{(-1)!!=1}, and zero odd
moments. The first moments are
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[y^4\mid x]&=x^4+6x^2+3,\\
\mathbb E[z^4\mid x]&=x^4+12x^2+12.
\end{aligned}}
For the second coefficient, the same pairings give
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}(y^4\mid x)
 &=16x^6+168x^4\\
 &\quad+384x^2+96,\\
\operatorname{Var}(z^4\mid x)
 &=32x^6+672x^4\\
 &\quad+3072x^2+1536,\\
\operatorname{Cov}(y^4,z^4\mid x)
 &=16x^6+216x^4\\
 &\quad+600x^2+168.
\end{aligned}}
For instance these mixed moments use
#{\mathbb E\eta^2=1}, #{\mathbb E(\eta+\xi)^2=2} and
#{\mathbb E[\eta(\eta+\xi)]=1}. The term #{x^4} is constant at
fixed #{x}, so
##{\begin{aligned}
a&=\frac{x^4}{8}+\frac{3x^2}{4}+\frac58,\\
b&=\frac{5x^6}{36}+\frac{53x^4}{24}
   +\frac{97x^2}{12}+\frac{41}{12}.
\end{aligned}}
Substitute these into #{F_\lambda=\lambda a-\lambda^2b/2+O(\lambda^3)}.}
}

\p{Let #{\mu_Q} be the [normalized Gaussian law](fdip-0005), and
let #{J} be a formal scalar source for the retained coordinate. Define
##{\begin{aligned}
W(J,\lambda)
={}&\log\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}e^{Jx-\lambda V}\\
 &-\log\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}e^{-\lambda V}.
\end{aligned}}
Each coefficient in the two formal variables #{J,\lambda} is a finite Gaussian
moment. Thus #{W(0,\lambda)=0}. Coefficients use ordinary powers
of #{\lambda}; four source derivatives multiply the coefficient
of #{J^4} by #{4!}. The
\newvocab{connected four-point function} in this scalar example is
##{\begin{aligned}
G_c^{(4)}(\lambda)
 &=\left.\frac{\partial^4W}{\partial J^4}\right|_{J=0}\\
 &=-3\lambda+101\lambda^2+O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
}

\proof{
\p{Write
##{\mathbb E_\lambda[P]=
 \frac{\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}[P e^{-\lambda V}]}
      {\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}e^{-\lambda V}}}
as a formal ratio. After eliminating #{y,z}, both numerator and
denominator are expectations for #{x\sim N(0,1)} with weight
##{e^{-F_\lambda(x)}
 =1-\lambda a(x)+\frac{\lambda^2}{2}(a(x)^2+b(x))
   +O(\lambda^3).}
The scalar #{c_C} cancels from this ratio, but the constant terms
in #{a,b} are included before the division. Gaussian moments give
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[e^{-F_\lambda}]&=
 1-\frac74\lambda+\frac{1351}{96}\lambda^2+O(\lambda^3),\\
\mathbb E[x^2e^{-F_\lambda}]&=
 1-\frac{19}{4}\lambda+\frac{1981}{32}\lambda^2+O(\lambda^3),\\
\mathbb E[x^4e^{-F_\lambda}]&=
 3-\frac{105}{4}\lambda+\frac{14799}{32}\lambda^2+O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
Dividing yields
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\lambda[x^2]&=
 1-3\lambda+\frac{511}{12}\lambda^2+O(\lambda^3),\\
\mathbb E_\lambda[x^4]&=
 3-21\lambda+\frac{767}{2}\lambda^2+O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
Sign reversal makes the odd moments zero and
#{W(-J,\lambda)=W(J,\lambda)}. Expanding the logarithm in #{J}
therefore gives
##{G_c^{(4)}=\mathbb E_\lambda[x^4]
 -3\bigl(\mathbb E_\lambda[x^2]\bigr)^2,}
which is the asserted coefficient.}
}

\p{These are formal expansions. For real #{\lambda\geq0}, the
original interaction is nonnegative, so its exact partition
integral is positive and bounded by the Gaussian normalizer.
Polynomial moments remain finite. A real source also gives a finite
integral, since the linear tilt of a positive Gaussian is integrable.
None of this asserts convergence of the formal series at zero.
For every fixed #{\lambda>0}, exponentiating only the displayed
second-order #{F_\lambda} instead gives a nonintegrable retained
weight: its leading term is #{-5\lambda^2x^6/72}, so
#{e^{-x^2/2-F_\lambda^{(2)}(x)}} grows at infinity. Here
#{F_\lambda^{(2)}} denotes the displayed polynomial through
#{\lambda^2}.}

\p{The Gaussian moment rule is
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.1, p. 29}{etingof2024mathematical}.
The connected-logarithm interpretation is
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.11, p. 38}{etingof2024mathematical}, with
#{g_4=-\lambda} and the zero-source logarithm subtracted. The
rational coefficients above are the explicit calculation for this
supplied model. [Stationary elimination](fdip-0048) retains its
sextic term but omits the fluctuation-generated lower degrees.}
